TEST 38418 -- frame forwarding rules: unicast & broadcast
===========================================================================
Run: 2026-09-22 12:44:00  tb470
Ingress port2.0.2 (tb470 eth2).  Observation point: tb470 eth1, reached only via
DUT port1.0.2 -> x230.  A frame counted on eth1 left the DUT by a DIFFERENT port
than it entered, which is what 'flooded' means here.

--- teach the DUT that 00:00:5e:00:53:01 lives on the INGRESS port ---
show mac address-table
VLAN port             mac            fwd 
1    port2.0.2    0000.5e00.5301   forward       dynamic
1    CPU          0000.cd37.0d6f   forward       static 
1    port1.0.2    00f0.4d00.7716   forward       dynamic
1    port2.0.2    00f0.4d00.7717   forward       dynamic
10   CPU          0000.cd37.0d6f   forward       static 
10   po1          0200.cd40.0394   forward       dynamic
4092 CPU          7eaa.88e6.b933   forward       static 
4092 CPU          a655.c628.eee1   forward       static 
IE520-stk#    1    port2.0.2    0000.5e00.5301   forward       dynamic

--- 1. unicast to an UNKNOWN destination MAC -> must be FLOODED ---
    seen on eth1: 10/10   expect flooded (>0)   PASS

--- 2. unicast to a KNOWN destination MAC that lives on the INGRESS port ---
    the DUT knows 00:00:5e:00:53:01 is on port2.0.2, the same port the frame arrives on,
    so it must NOT be flooded out port1.0.2.
    seen on eth1:  0/10   expect NOT flooded (0)  PASS

--- 3. broadcast -> must be FLOODED ---
    seen on eth1: 10/10   expect flooded (>0)   PASS

VERDICT: PASS -- unknown unicast floods, known unicast does not, broadcast floods

SCOPE: the case says 'flooded out ALL ports in the VLAN' and asks to repeat on
access and trunk ports. This bench has exactly TWO cabled ports in the test
VLAN (port2.0.2 ingress, port1.0.2 egress), so 'all ports' is exercised as 'the other port'.
The three forwarding RULES are each distinguished, which is what the case is
about; fan-out across many ports is not, and would need more cabled ports.
